# Mapping Components

## Context
Prototypes include map pages for drawing a red line boundary and checking overlap with EDP (Environmental Delivery Plan) areas. There are two patterns in the repo:

1. **Production map component (preferred, `nrf-quote-7`)** – the same `@defra/interactive-map` npm package the live service uses, loaded through the Prototype Kit's plugin asset serving. Use this for new work.
2. **Legacy stacks** – a hand-copied older alpha of the same package plus `app/assets/javascripts/components/map/` (`nrf-estimate-5/6`, `nrf-quote-6`), and MapLibre + mapbox-gl-draw scripts (`nrf-estimate-1..4`, `nrf-quote-4`). Leave these alone unless you are migrating a journey.

## Production map pattern (nrf-quote-7)
- **Package**: `@defra/interactive-map` is pinned to an exact alpha version in `package.json`. It ships a `govuk-prototype-kit.config.json`, so the kit serves its UMD bundles, CSS and page templates at `/plugin-assets/%40defra%2Finteractive-map/...`. **Never vendor or copy its files.** To upgrade, bump the version and re-test.
- **Layout**: map pages extend `layouts/interactive-map.html`. It links the plugin CSS, loads the UMD `<script>` tags in the right order (core, maplibre provider, interact, draw, datasets, map-key, map-styles, scale-bar, search) and exposes a `mapScripts` block for the page's `<script type="module">`. Do not add `defer`/`async` to the UMD tags: the module script relies on them having populated `window.defra` first.
- **Client glue**: `app/assets/javascripts/interactive-map/` is a near-verbatim port of the production frontend's `src/client/javascripts/map/` (nrf-frontend), written as browser ES modules with no bundler. Keep the file structure and names identical to production so the two can be diffed; the only intended differences are reading the library from `window.defra`, using `console` instead of the production logger, and submitting the page form instead of a JSON save endpoint.
- **Basemaps**: style JSON lives in `app/assets/data/vts/` (served at `/public/data/vts/`, the same path production uses). Ordnance Survey styles go through `app/routes/os-base-map.js`, which adds `OS_API_KEY` server-side and rewrites OS URLs so the key never reaches the browser. Without a key only the keyless Satellite and Streets (OpenFreeMap) styles are offered (`shared-helpers/styles.js`).
- **EDP overlays**: the datasets config is identical to production's and requests `/impact-assessor-map/tiles/{layer}/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt`. `app/routes/tileserver-proxy.js` slices those tiles on demand with geojson-vt from `app/lib/map/edp-data.js` (production layer names, every zoom level), or proxies the real service when `IMPACT_ASSESSOR_BASE_URL` is set. `edp-data.js` groups the nutrient catchments in `app/assets/map-layers/` by designated site and dissolves each group into one EDP outline (the live Broads/Wensum EDP uses production's exact label; other groups are synthetic EDPs so the journey works anywhere), and holds the excluded areas (`edp_excluded_areas.geojson`: River Wensum SAC, The Broads SAC and Broadland Ramsar boundaries from Natural England open data, OGL v3). Boundary checks report whole EDPs, one entry per plan, like production.
- **Boundary check API**: the page's hook registers `POST {basePath}/api/boundary/check` taking `{ geometry }` (GeoJSON Polygon) and returning the production shape: `{ boundaryGeometryWgs84, boundaryGeometryOriginal, boundaryMetadata: { area: { hectares, acres }, perimeter: { kilometres, miles }, bounds: { bottomLeft, topRight }, centre }, intersectingEdps: [{ label, type }], intersectingExcludedAreas: [] }`. The map's "Boundary information" panel renders this.
- **Saving**: the panel's "Save and continue" writes the check payload into the hidden `#boundary-data` input and submits `#map-form`. The hook's `validate` accepts that payload (plus GeoJSON and the legacy `{ center, coordinates }` shape), `process` re-runs the EDP check server-side and stores the session shape below, and `journey.yaml` decides the next page.

## Legacy map pages
- **Layout**: `layouts/map.html` (full-screen `main`, no footer).
- **Libraries**: vendored `@defra/interactive-map` 0.0.10 UMD under `app/assets/javascripts/vendor/interactive-map/` with `components/map/init.js`, or MapLibre GL + Mapbox GL Draw from CDN; turf.js for geometry.
- **API**: `POST {basePath}/api/check-edp-intersection` taking `{ coordinates }`.

## Data stored for the boundary (all patterns)
- **redlineBoundaryPolygon**: `{ center, coordinates (unclosed ring), intersections: { nutrient, gcn }, intersectingCatchment }`; the production pattern adds `boundaryGeojson` (the full check payload).
- **intersectingCatchment**: first nutrient EDP name, used for branching, summary and emails.
- **hasRedlineBoundaryFile** / **mapReferrer**: whether the user uploaded a file; where the map's back link goes.

## EDP data and performance
- GeoJSON in `app/assets/map-layers/`. Load it once at module startup and cache it (see `loadEdpData()` in `app/lib/nrf-quote-7/hooks.js`); never `readFileSync` per request.
- Use turf for intersection: `turf.booleanIntersects(boundaryPolygon, edpFeature)`; collect intersecting EDP names for redirect logic and display.
- Vector tiles are MBTiles in `tileserver/data/mbtiles/`; regenerate with `npm run tiles:convert`.

## Validation and copy
- Submitting without a boundary: "Draw a red line boundary to continue" (match the prompt / page file).
- Page title: "Draw a red line boundary – Get a quote for Nature Restoration Fund levy – GOV.UK".

## Examples

### ✅ Good – production-shaped boundary check in a hook
```javascript
router.post(`${journey.basePath}/api/boundary/check`, (req, res) => {
  const failureReason = validatePolygonGeometry(req.body.geometry)
  if (failureReason) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Draw a valid red line boundary to check it', failureReason })
  }
  return res.json(checkBoundary(req.body.geometry))
})
```

### ❌ Poor – copying library files into app/assets
Do not copy `node_modules/@defra/interactive-map/**` into `app/assets/javascripts/vendor/`. Reference `/plugin-assets/%40defra%2Finteractive-map/...` instead so upgrades are a version bump.

### ❌ Poor – reading GeoJSON on every request
```javascript
router.get(ROUTES.MAP, (req, res) => {
  const geojson = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(__dirname, '../assets/map-layers/catchments.geojson'), 'utf8'))
  res.render(TEMPLATES.MAP, { data: req.session.data, catchments: geojson })
})
```
Prefer loading at module load and reusing a cached variable.
